Z||000000||fierohoho||06-30-2001||07:53 PM||||Well I replaced my plugs ('84 2.5L) and after cruising the forum I couldn't figure out why everyone was gaping their plugs at .060 and not the .045 like it said on the sticker under my hood, until the freaking light bulb went on and I realized I had replaced the hood with one from a V6 so I could put the wing on. No harm done but the hassle but still, <P>DOH! Stupid,Stupid,Stupid,DOH!<P>------------------<BR>'84 SE 2.5l<BR>'85 Coupe 2.5l now getting a 3800<BR>'86 SE 2.5l||||reg||8
Z||000001||rogergarrison||06-30-2001||08:41 PM||||NEVER ever trust stickers. Get a manual. I learned about that a while back. GM cars put there paint codes usually on the cardnoard spare tire cover in trunk. Well I do a lot of late model cars for a big dealer and I figured out after getting wrong paint a bunch of times, that a lot of them were lease and rental company returns and when they cleaned them up as a group, they simply had switched a lot of them by mistake.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||07-01-2001||08:08 PM||||Far as I know.... only the 87-88 DIS 4 cyl uses an 060 gap. The HEI motors all use smaller gaps. ||||reg||1
